#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082925 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "b293a58f-0903-5ee7-ab4b-3cbc2e5e4b3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-560" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of  the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010575/assets/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= x^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =   \\dfrac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 }\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+9x}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(4+9x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}\\sqrt{4+9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 } = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution $u= 4+9x.$ Then, $\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\textrm{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ u & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{16}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{\\int }_{4}^{16}{u}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([(16)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]-[(4)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([64]-[8]) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "cf9adf09-b14f-5b5b-a110-60354b93c241" :question/math-academy-id "e-561" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\displaystyle  \\int_{5}^{6} } \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve? \n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}$\n\n- $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5$\n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{x}{x^2-16} + 1$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL=\\int_{5}^{6}  \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[f'(x)]^2=\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(x) = \\pm \\dfrac {x}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083015 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/id #uuid "80794b09-e0f4-5bff-8c0c-8261638f9613" :question/math-academy-id "e-7268" :question/problem "What is the arc length of the curve $y = \\arccos{x}$ on $\\left[0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right]?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= \\arccos{x} ,$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{2-{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
